// THE ABUSE DIRECTION of the same rule's EXEMPTION. Widening a name's scope to
// the enclosing `impl<'a>` header is a PERMISSIVE change, so the question it
// has to answer is what it stops refusing. Before 2026-08-28 this program was
// refused as "use of undeclared lifetime name ''a' in outlives clause" — a
// refusal for a reason that was not true: `'a` IS in scope here. Its sound
// mirror (`where 'b: 'a`) was refused by the identical wrong reason and is now
// admitted, in pass/bc_ltscope_impl_legal_shapes::stash_sound.
//
// This one must STAY refused, and for the real reason: `self.r` is `&'a i32`
// and `o` is `&'b i32`, so storing `o` into it needs `'b: 'a`. The clause
// declares the opposite.
//
// ⚠ THE PINNED DIAGNOSTIC MOVED ON 2026-08-28, AND IT MOVED TO THE SITE THIS
// HEADER ALREADY NAMED. It used to be E0597 raised at the CALL — the first
// place a short-lived borrow was seen to escape. `lower_place_assign` now runs
// the same check_variance its sibling `lower_deref_assign` has run since B68,
// so `self.r = o` is refused AT THE ASSIGNMENT, one phase earlier, naming the
// two regions instead of the symptom. The program's verdict is unchanged: rc 1
// before, rc 1 after. Only the reason got truer.
//
// ⚠ AND THE CALL-SITE E0597 IS NOT ORPHANED BY THAT. It is kept live by
// `bc_ltscope_wrong_direction_callsite_fail`, which is THIS PROGRAM with the
// bound turned the SOUND way round (`where 'b: 'a`): the assignment is then
// legal, nothing refuses it in sema, and the escape is caught where it always
// was. The two are a pair — one pins each phase — because a new sensor that
// silently takes over an old guard's only fixture is how a guard dies.
package bc_ltscope_wrong_direction_fail;

struct H<'a> { r: &'a i32 }

impl<'a> H<'a> {
    fn stash<'b>(self: &mut H<'a>, o: &'b i32) where 'a: 'b { self.r = o; }
}

fn main() -> i32 {
    let v: i32 = 1i32;
    let mut h: H = H { r: &v };
    { let tmp: i32 = 2i32; h.stash(&tmp); }   // ERROR: 'tmp' does not live long enough
    return *h.r - 1i32;
}
